(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-3-food-safety-quiz"]={"title":"Food Safety — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which household exposure is a known source of Salmonella?","choices":["Keeping live backyard hens","Moldy bread left in the pantry","Digging in garden soil","Raw honey kept in the pantry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Live poultry such as backyard hens is a Salmonella source alongside poultry products, eggs and pet food, which fits its association mainly with poultry and aquatic reptiles. Raw honey and garden soil, by contrast, are where clostridial spores are found. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 17, 18, 23, 25 and 31"},{"q":"A laboratory wants to confirm Escherichia coli in a water sample, not link it to other isolates. Which tool is often used?","choices":["A PulseNet database search","A federal food recall search","Eosin methylene blue plates","Pulsed-field gel electrophoresis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Eosin methylene blue plates are often used to confirm Escherichia coli in samples such as food and water. Escherichia coli is an indicator organism associated with human and animal feces, so confirming it points to fecal contamination. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 14, 15, 34 and 35"},{"q":"How do recorded counts of foodborne illness compare with the true number of cases?","choices":["Low for viruses, accurate for bacteria","They are probably too low","They are probably too high","They match closely, thanks to surveillance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The yearly estimate of foodborne illness is described as probably an under-reported number, and Campylobacter jejuni illness in particular is very much under-reported. Many people who fall ill are never counted, so recorded counts likely understate the true burden. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 9, 21 and 34"},{"q":"When is a person with hepatitis A most contagious?","choices":["At the height of the symptoms","About two weeks after symptoms","About two weeks before symptoms","On the very first day of symptoms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With the fecal-oral hepatitis viruses, a person is most contagious about two weeks before symptoms appear. That silent window is why handwashing, diaper hygiene, thorough cooking and clean drinking water matter, and a vaccine exists for suspected exposure. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which kinds of Escherichia coli strains carry numerous toxins that make them harmful?","choices":["Salt-tolerant strains from estuarine water","Fermenting strains used in yogurt making","Enterotoxigenic and enterohemorrhagic strains","Spore-forming strains from soil and honey"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Escherichia coli is not always pathogenic, but its bad strains can carry numerous toxins; enterotoxigenic and enterohemorrhagic strains are the examples. The O157:H7 serotype is one of the worst culprits in outbreaks. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 7, 15, 25 and 29"},{"q":"Which bacteria are involved in turning milk into yogurt?","choices":["Escherichia coli and Salmonella","Lactobacillus and Streptococcus","Saccharomyces and Clostridium","Spirulina and other cyanobacteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Yogurt is a type of fermented milk made with bacteria such as Lactobacillus and Streptococcus. Cheese-making, by contrast, draws on yeast, bacteria and molds (multicellular fungi), and kefir, koumiss and buttermilk are other fermented milks. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 4, 7, 8, 15, 17 and 31"},{"q":"A person develops foodborne vibriosis. Which food most likely carried it?","choices":["Peanut butter","Raw oysters","Soft cheese","Fried rice"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The foodborne route of vibriosis runs through seafood: shellfish, crabs, raw seafood and oysters. Vibrio species are salt-tolerant and live in ocean and estuarine waters, the source of those foods. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 29, 23, 28 and 18"},{"q":"In the food infection pathway, which step lets bacteria multiply in the food before it is eaten?","choices":["Thorough cooking to 165 F","Toxin release into the food","Ingestion of the cooked food","Improper cooking technique"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In food infection, live cells in a food such as poultry come through improper cooking technique and multiply in the food; the larger number is then ingested and causes an intestinal infection. Salmonella and Listeria are the examples of this pattern. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 12 and 36 and image on Slide 12"},{"q":"Within a few hours of a summer picnic, several guests who ate fried rice develop vomiting and abdominal cramps. Which organism is the likely cause?","choices":["Clostridium botulinum","Bacillus cereus","Campylobacter jejuni","Listeria monocytogenes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bacillus cereus is a very common food poisoning agent, especially in summertime. Its emetic form is often associated with rice, and the intoxication has a rapid onset (6 hours or less) with vomiting and abdominal cramps that resolve on their own. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 28, 21, 23 and 27"},{"q":"Milk begins as a sterile mixture. How do microbes first get into it?","choices":["On leaving the animal and from utensils","They are already present in the udder","From yeast in human saliva","When cheese molds are added to it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Milk begins as a sterile mixture of water, minerals, protein, butterfat, lactose and other sugars; microbes are introduced as it exits the female mammal's body and by milking utensils. Once contaminated it is highly susceptible to microbial growth because of its nutrient density. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 5 and 7"},{"q":"Which food-handling rule is given for preventing Bacillus cereus food poisoning?","choices":["Preserve food in salt brine with vinegar","Keep hot foods hot and cold foods cold","Skip oysters in months without an R","Get a vaccine after a suspected exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bacillus cereus is a very common food poisoning agent, especially in summertime, and the rule given against it is to keep hot foods hot and cold foods cold. Its intoxication starts rapidly, within 6 hours or less, with vomiting and cramps. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 28, 29, 33 and 6"},{"q":"Escherichia coli is found everywhere, including inside healthy humans. What explains its presence without disease?","choices":["It survives as dormant spores","Its illness takes weeks to appear","It spoils food rather than infecting","Not every strain is pathogenic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Escherichia coli is found everywhere, including inside humans, but it is not always pathogenic and has many good traits too. Illness comes from the bad strains, which can carry numerous toxins (for example enterotoxigenic or enterohemorrhagic strains). — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 15, 17, 25 and 31"},{"q":"Which feature describes typhoid fever, the most severe enteric fever?","choices":["Growth even at refrigerator temperatures","A tiny dose of a few particles","Invasion of the gut wall, then blood and nodes","A preformed toxin that paralyzes muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Typhoid fever, caused by Salmonella typhi, is the most severe enteric fever, and it is marked by invasion of the intestinal wall, with possible spread to the blood and lymph nodes. Other forms of salmonellosis include gastroenteritis, septicemia, fever, shock and kidney dysfunction. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 17, 23, 27 and 33"},{"q":"At what point does Clostridium botulinum start producing its toxin?","choices":["When its spores germinate","When its cells form spores","After live cells reach the gut","When it invades the gut wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When Clostridium botulinum spores germinate, they can start producing toxin, the botulinum toxin. The spores come from soil, plants or honey, and even if only a few bacteria are present they can produce plenty of the toxin. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 27, 25, 12 and 17"},{"q":"Foodborne Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora or Giardia typically causes which illness?","choices":["Usually rapid bloody diarrhea with fever","Enteric fever spreading to the blood","Paralysis of muscles in the relaxed position","Mild diarrhea that is usually self-limiting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The common foodborne protozoans, Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Giardia and Entamoeba, can all cause mild diarrhea that is usually self-limiting. It is associated with uncooked fruits and vegetables or drinking water. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 32, 21, 17 and 27"},{"q":"Why is Mary Mallon, known as Typhoid Mary, remembered in food safety?","choices":["She was the first person to die of typhoid","She traced Salmonella to poultry and reptiles","She kept cooking despite warnings, causing outbreaks","Her backyard hens spread Salmonella to others"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mary Mallon was warned and given an explanation, yet she persisted in preparing food and caused multiple deadly outbreaks. Typhoid fever, caused by Salmonella typhi, is the most severe enteric fever, so a single person handling food could spread it widely. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 17, 18 and 20 and image on Slide 20"},{"q":"Which foodborne bacterium is a leading cause of bacterial diarrheal disease each year in the United States?","choices":["Listeria monocytogenes","Clostridium botulinum","Vibrio vulnificus","Campylobacter jejuni"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Campylobacter jejuni is a leading cause of bacterial diarrheal disease each year in the United States, especially in children, and the illness is very much under-reported. Its infectious dose is under 500 bacterial cells. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 21, 23, 27 and 29"},{"q":"Which food safety responsibility rests with the Food and Drug Administration and the United States Department of Agriculture rather than with local and state health departments?","choices":["Conducting food safety surveillance","Investigating illness outbreaks","Hosting the PulseNet database","Enforcing food safety law and policy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Local and state health departments, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the Food and Drug Administration and the United States Department of Agriculture all conduct surveillance and investigate outbreaks, but the Food and Drug Administration and the United States Department of Agriculture are in charge of law enforcement and policy enactment. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 9, 34 and 35"},{"q":"Which set of foods is most vulnerable to Listeria monocytogenes?","choices":["Eggs, peanut butter and pet food","Bread, cereals and acidic juices","Soft cheeses, deli meats, hot dogs","Rice dishes kept warm on summer days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The foods most vulnerable to Listeria monocytogenes are soft cheeses, raw fruit and vegetables, raw or undercooked meats, deli meats, seafood, smoked seafood, hot dogs and preserved meats. It also grows well at refrigerator temperatures, anywhere from 1 to 40 C. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 23, 28, 18 and 31"},{"q":"After a preformed toxin is swallowed in food intoxication, where does it act?","choices":["The intestine, where it starts an infection","The liver and spleen, via the blood","Lymph nodes, after invading the gut wall","Target organs such as brain and muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In food intoxication the ingested toxin passes through the gut to its target organs, such as the brain and the muscles. Botulinum toxin is an example: it paralyzes muscles in the relaxed position, which can lead to respiratory failure. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 12, 17, 24 and 27 and image on Slide 12"},{"q":"Which two Clostridium species are not food-related?","choices":["Clostridium botulinum and Clostridium tetani","Clostridium perfringens and Clostridium difficile","Clostridium tetani and Clostridium difficile","Clostridium botulinum and Clostridium perfringens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Of the notable clostridia, Clostridium tetani and Clostridium difficile are the two marked as not food-related. Clostridium botulinum and Clostridium perfringens are the food species, botulinum through the toxin behind foodborne botulism. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 25 and 27"},{"q":"Which agency runs PulseNet?","choices":["Food and Drug Administration","United States Department of Agriculture","Local and state health departments","Centers for Disease Control and Prevention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pulsed-field gel electrophoresis pattern of each microbial isolate is added to PulseNet, a database run through the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and reached from its website. It helps find outbreak causes and problems at food processing plants. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 34, 35 and 9"},{"q":"Which group is named among those at greatest risk for both salmonellosis and listeriosis?","choices":["Young children","Immunocompromised people","Elderly people","Pregnant women"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Young children and the elderly are at greatest risk for salmonellosis. Pregnant women, unborn children, premature babies, elderly people and immunocompromised people are at greatest risk for listeriosis. The elderly are the group on both lists. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 17 and 23"},{"q":"Leftover cooked chicken has sat in the refrigerator for an unknown time and smells normal. What should be done with it?","choices":["Rinse it, then serve it cold","Toss it out rather than risk it","Eat it, since it still smells normal","Taste a small bite first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When in doubt, toss it out. Refrigeration does not settle the question: Listeria monocytogenes grows well at refrigerator temperatures, and with Campylobacter jejuni from poultry even one drop of raw poultry fluid can cause illness. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 36, 3, 21 and 23"},{"q":"Why does norovirus spread so readily in daycares and nursing homes?","choices":["A few viral particles can infect","It grows well in chilled food","Its spores survive in soil","Its toxin forms before it is eaten"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The infectious dose of norovirus is only a few viral particles, and it spreads person to person through feces and vomitus. Together these drive outbreaks in daycares, nursing homes and cruise ships. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 33, 23, 25 and 12"},{"q":"Foodborne protozoan diarrhea is associated with which sources?","choices":["Uncooked produce and drinking water","Undercooked pork and wild venison","Raw shellfish from salty estuaries","Raw honey and the soil around plants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Protozoans such as Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Giardia and Entamoeba cause mild diarrhea associated with uncooked fruits and vegetables or drinking water. Ready-to-eat salad bags are a common culprit of foodborne illness. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 32, 29 and 25"},{"q":"Where are Vibrio species such as Vibrio cholerae and Vibrio vulnificus found?","choices":["Poultry and aquatic reptiles","Ocean and estuarine waters","Birds, mammals and soil","Soil, plants and honey"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vibrio species, including Vibrio cholerae and Vibrio vulnificus, are salt-tolerant bacteria found in ocean and estuarine waters. People acquire them from that water, swallowed or through wounds, or from seafood such as shellfish, crabs and oysters. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 29, 23, 25 and 17"},{"q":"In bread making, what do microbial enzymes acting on gluten give the dough?","choices":["Elasticity that makes it workable","Rise from carbon dioxide gas","Flavor from organic acids","Preservation, as a salt brine does"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Conditioning is one of the three microbial functions in bread making: microbial enzymes act on gluten to give the dough elasticity, making it pliable and workable. Leavening (by gases) and flavor and aroma (by organic acids and alcohols) are the other two functions. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 4 and 6"},{"q":"Besides poultry meat, which routes carry Campylobacter jejuni to people in its transmission cycle?","choices":["Contaminated water and milk from cattle","Vomitus and person-to-person contact","Spores in soil and raw honey","Wounds exposed to seawater"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Campylobacter jejuni transmission cycle reaches people by three routes: poultry meat, contaminated water, and milk from cattle. Poultry still needs particular care, since even one drop of raw poultry fluid can cause illness. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 25, 29 and 33 and image on Slide 21"},{"q":"How does Listeria monocytogenes cause illness after a contaminated food is eaten?","choices":["Live cells are ingested and multiply","Its preformed toxin reaches target organs","Its spores germinate and make toxin","It spoils the food's flavor and smell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Listeria is an example of food infection: live cells are ingested and multiply, often after improper cooking lets the bacteria multiply in the food, and the result is an intestinal infection. From the intestine it can spread through a lymph node and the bloodstream. — PAJ5200.FoodSafety-micro.pptx, Slide 12, 3, 24, 25 and 27 and image on Slide 12"}]};
